  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 81+16(y-7)^{2} & =9\\cdot 16 \\\\ 81+16(y-7)^{2} & =144 \\\\ 16(y-7)^{2} & =63 \\\\ (y-7)^{2} & =\\frac{63}{16} \\\\ y-7 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{16}} \\\\ y-7 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ y & =7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,7-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4})$ and $(0,7+\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
